, 12204, 4821, 12655, 13, 1921, 264, 13852, 1496, 12692, 13775, 3575, 2493, 599, 220, 16, 1915, 64456, 791, 2018, 314, 4779, 32288, 13, 733, 421, 1105, 11, 279, 4779, 628, 7892, 1132, 220, 16, 1915, 64456, 791, 2018, 11, 748, 279, 21966, 628, 13057, 958, 1132, 883, 220, 16, 1915, 2492, 21597, 791, 2018, 314, 1141, 220, 16, 15, 15, 1915, 2492, 51888, 13, 561, 2645, 314, 279, 12204, 2279, 22294, 26750, 11, 1040, 264, 28736, 440, 264, 6552, 42558, 694, 264, 13280, 65949, 5817, 13, 271, 1919, 1503, 524, 2977, 279, 21966, 369, 10548, 13, 1049, 3254, 279, 51797, 369, 9859, 364, 2166, 1691, 795, 6977, 7463, 310, 279, 2420, 32288, 13, 4188, 3739, 466, 44937, 1144, 9780, 314, 28876, 11, 16547, 11, 466, 18465, 795, 13, 24235, 513, 777, 12204, 71065, 321, 628, 958, 279, 21966, 2577, 13, 271, 4422, 382, 3372, 440, 411, 303, 3679, 5337], "content": "We need to answer user: \"Explain to a curious high-school student, in about 400 words, why a graphics card can be limited by memory bandwidth rather than by how fast it computes. Use one concrete analogy, give a simple worked example with made-up but plausible numbers, and finish by explaining what engineers do about it.\"\n\nNeed produce final about 400 words. Need no preamble. Need likely exactly around 400 words? \"about 400 words\". Let's craft ~400 words. Need concrete analogy, worked example plausible numbers, finish engineers do about it.\n\nLet's draft and count. Need high-school friendly.\n\nDraft:\n\"Imagine a graphics card is a kitchen. The GPU is the chef, and memory is the pantry. The chef can chop, mix, and fry very fast, but if the pantry door is narrow, only a few ingredients can be carried in at a time. The chef may stand idle, waiting for the next batch of flour, eggs, or oil. That waiting is memory bandwidth: the rate at which data can move between memory and the GPU.\n\nA GPU does not just compute one number at a time. It computes millions of tiny operations, such as shading pixels, checking textures, or updating positions. Many of those operations need data from memory. If the data arrives too slowly, the fast compute units cannot stay busy. This is called being memory-bound.\n\nHere is a simple example. Suppose a GPU can do 100 trillion floating-point operations per second, or 100 TFLOPS. Imagine each operation needs, on average, 1 byte of data from memory. Then the GPU would need 100 terabytes per second of memory bandwidth to keep every compute unit busy. But a typical high-end graphics card might have 1 terabyte per second of memory bandwidth. In that case, the memory can supply only 1 terabyte per second, so the GPU can effectively use only about 1 teraflop per second of its 100 teraflops. The rest of the compute power sits idle, like a chef with a huge stove but a tiny pantry door.\n\nThis does not mean the GPU is broken. It means the workload is asking for too much data movement compared to the available bandwidth. Some games or simulations need lots of textures, geometry, or simulation data. Others are more compute-heavy and can use the GPU better.\n\nEngineers deal with this in several ways", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0006973790586926043], [760, -8.014647483825684]], [[1144, -1.7762342395144515e-05], [1902, -11.67766284942627]], [[310, -0.6739901304244995], [4087, -0.7691554427146912]], [[4087, -0.7674993872642517], [5707, -0.772756040096283]], [[1156, -0.021503157913684845], [303, -3.866502523422241]], [[25, -0.44964149594306946], [579, -1.136771559715271]], [[328, -0.5429568886756897], [10033, -1.2197872400283813]], [[814, -6.354056677082554e-05], [2523, -10.165297508239746]], [[20139, -5.841272468387615e-06], [10083, -12.541191101074219]], [[310, -0.00011242067557759583], [2423, -9.248586654663086]], [[264, -0.00012577371671795845], [21520, -9.228048324584961]], [[21520, -0.0003243018581997603], [2754, -9.330631256103516]], [[1496, -1.1682578588079195e-05], [4962, -12.964709281921387]], [[33541, -0.00015844170411583036], [54087, -9.629175186157227]], [[5286, -3.00411